          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I had flu two weeks ago and I am still exhausted. Is prolonged tiredness afterwards a concern, and what does guidance describe about antivirals and vaccination?",
          "answer": "If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice (condition-copd, condition-influenza, lifestyle-hypertension-activity, condition-osteoarthritis, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals).\n\nProlonged tiredness after the flu is not described as an emergency, and the system found no emergency pattern for this symptom in its red-flag table (condition-copd, condition-influenza, lifestyle-hypertension-activity, condition-osteoarthritis, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals). This table covers a fixed list of presentations, and a symptom it does not list can still be serious (condition-copd, condition-influenza, lifestyle-hypertension-activity, condition-osteoarthritis, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals). The information available to the research specialist did not address whether prolonged tiredness after the flu is a concern (condition-influenza, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, condition-copd, coding-icd10-chapter-10-respiratory, guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy).\n\nRegarding antivirals for influenza, treatment is most effective when started within 48 hours of symptom onset, and its benefit decreases after that window (Guideline: Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment). Treatment is recommended as early as possible for anyone hospitalized with suspected or confirmed influenza, anyone with severe, complicated, or progressive illness, and anyone at higher risk of complications (Guideline: Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment). For otherwise healthy outpatients presenting within 48 hours, treatment is a reasonable option based on shared decision-making, offering a modest reduction in symptom duration (Guideline: Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment). Treatment started after 48 hours is still considered appropriate for hospitalized and severely ill patients, where observational evidence supports a benefit beyond the outpatient window (Guideline: Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment).\n\nHowever, for low-risk outpatients, guidance on antivirals differs (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals). While some guidance suggests antivirals can be a reasonable option for reducing symptoms, other guidance and independent reviews are more cautious (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals). These sources note that the effect on how long symptoms last is modest, and it is not well established whether antivirals prevent complications or hospital stays in this group, suggesting that routine use may not be justified (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals). For high-risk patients, recommendations for antiviral use are consistent (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals).\n\nSpecific guidance on vaccination for influenza could not be retrieved by the research specialist (condition-influenza, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, condition-copd, coding-icd10-chapter-10-respiratory, guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
